{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Completion state\n\nThis epic is complete. It replaces the fragmented Jira-specific operator surface with one provider-neutral, Python-first bridge surface while retaining every live legacy contract needed by existing workflows and environments. Twenty descendants are closed; their code is merged, including final Gerrit change 1595, and the completed tree is present at ref `694cf894def36241724e7049e3308d782037ef6d`.\n\n## Product and compatibility decisions\n\n- The canonical noun is `bridge`; the design is intentionally tracker-neutral.\n- `bridge preview` and `bridge sync` are primary. `reconcile` remains an explicit compatibility adapter with its historical safe dry-run default.\n- Canonical verbs are setup, check-access, fsck, status, preview, sync, pause, and resume.\n- `bridge-status` remains a compatibility entrypoint. The audited-dead `purge-bridge` spelling remains removed.\n- Canonical exits are 0, 1, and 2. Legacy adapters retain historical 3, 4, and 75 translations where older workflows and environments depend on them.\n- Canonical operational selection uses `--max-changes`, `--only`, `--except`, and `--max-age`, with user-facing help that does not expose engine internals.\n- Pause state is remote-persisted, includes a reason, and remains readable by mixed-version fleets.\n- Destructive repair and normal bridge operation no longer require the GitHub-specific `gh` CLI.\n\nThe default inversion is deliberate and covered through real entrypoints: argument-less `reconcile` remains non-mutating, while argument-less `bridge sync` is the canonical mutating command. Compatibility is implemented as explicit adapters rather than a naive alias.\n\n## Three interfaces\n\nAll supported interfaces expose the bridge functionality through shared Python services:\n\n- Python library: public imports provide the bridge operations and preserve legacy reconcile behavior.\n- CLI: `rebar bridge ...` is canonical; retained commands and flags normalize through compatibility adapters.\n- MCP server: MCP tools expose the same bridge capabilities and route through the same library/core contracts.\n\nAutomated workflows use the same Python core. Reads, preview, and status remain compatible while outbound sync/delivery selects its intended strictness explicitly. No surface has a private reimplementation of bridge semantics.\n\n## Three install paths\n\nThe feature works from all supported installation routes:\n\n- brew installs the packaged `rebar` CLI and Python implementation.\n- pip installs the same library and console entrypoints.\n- MCP-server installation runs the same bridge library/core through `rebar-mcp`.\n\nNo supported path depends on an editable checkout, repository-only helper, ambient Python, or a newly introduced shell script. Runtime ownership is Python; YAML, Jenkins, and GitLab files remain thin declarative wrappers.\n\n## Portable automation and delivery\n\nThe bridge is runner-neutral across local execution, GitHub Actions, Jenkins, GitLab CI, and bare environments. Provider wrappers share code-derived exit behavior, secrets vocabulary, and timeout policy. Full-depth execution succeeds; shallow history fails with a named diagnostic. Jenkins secret use avoids Groovy interpolation. GitLab configuration is validated offline against its vendored pinned schema. Required shellcheck coverage validates the unavoidable provider shell bodies as test artifacts; shellcheck is not a runtime dependency and bridge behavior is not implemented in shell.\n\nTickets-branch delivery is also Python-owned:\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/push.py` owns CAS retry, fetch-and-merge recovery, strict failure classification, and the terminal push budget.\n- The locked Python `commit-and-push` entrypoint owns staging, commit identity/subject, and delivery.\n- The bridge workflow invokes `rebar bridge run`; the canary invokes the Python push adapter once.\n- Workflow-local CAS loops and the stale raw-git exemption rationale are absent.\n- Existing Python, CLI, MCP, importer, and ticket-store callers remain best effort unless explicitly adapted.\n\n## ADR numbering and indexing\n\nADR 0092 records the bridge-primary vocabulary and explicit compatibility-adapter decision. It complies with the current ADR convention:\n\n- the filename is `docs/adr/0092-bridge-primary-vocabulary-compatibility-adapters.md`;\n- the heading uses the same unique four-digit number;\n- `docs/adr/README.md` contains the matching 0092 index entry;\n- the repository ADR collision/index gate recognizes the file;\n- later changes updated ADR 0092 and the older ADR 0027/0070 references without creating a duplicate or out-of-index decision.\n\n## Verification evidence\n\nThe merged descendant changes passed Gerrit LLM review and CI. The combined RED-first and held-out coverage includes:\n\n- real CLI entrypoint parity for canonical and legacy defaults;\n- Python-library and MCP facade parity;\n- canonical 0/1/2 and legacy 3/4/75 exit behavior;\n- pause/resume persistence, reason handling, and mixed-version reads;\n- local, GitHub, Jenkins, GitLab, bare, full-depth, and shallow-history contracts;\n- successful, contended, and rejected tickets-branch pushes in real temporary git repositories;\n- strict workflow failure and best-effort legacy caller controls;\n- documentation/help/schema/release-note stale-vocabulary sweeps;\n- ADR uniqueness and index coverage;\n- repository lint, typecheck, full tests, module-size, packaging, and workflow gates.\n\nThe former broken user-guide `--dry-run` example has regression coverage. The CLI whitelist is gone. Current workflows and documentation use canonical bridge vocabulary while compatibility tests keep older environments working.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] The canonical CLI exposes setup, check-access, fsck, status, preview, sync, pause, and resume under `rebar bridge`.\n- [x] Argument-less legacy `reconcile` remains dry-run.\n- [x] Argument-less canonical `bridge sync` remains live.\n- [x] Legacy `bridge-status` remains usable.\n- [x] Canonical exit codes are 0, 1, and 2.\n- [x] Historical 3, 4, and 75 translations remain available through legacy adapters.\n- [x] The Python library exposes the bridge surface.\n- [x] The CLI exposes the bridge surface.\n- [x] The MCP server exposes the bridge surface.\n- [x] brew installation executes the packaged bridge implementation.\n- [x] pip installation executes the packaged bridge implementation.\n- [x] MCP-server installation executes the packaged bridge implementation.\n- [x] Pause state is durable and mixed-version safe.\n- [x] Core operations have no required `gh` dependency.\n- [x] Local, GitHub, Jenkins, GitLab, and bare environments use the same runner-neutral core.\n- [x] Python core owns tickets-branch CAS delivery.\n- [x] No workflow-local CAS retry loop remains.\n- [x] ADR 0092 has a unique convention-compliant number.\n- [x] ADR 0092 is indexed in `docs/adr/README.md`.\n- [x] All 20 descendants are closed.\n- [x] Every merged code change passed both Gerrit review gates before submission.\n\n## Rollback and residual gaps\n\nThe migration is additive and has no persisted-schema backfill. Rollback must restore a workflow delivery implementation before reverting its Python strict adapter, and retained legacy adapters provide the operational fallback. Persistent pause readers accept old and new records, so downgrade remains safe.\n\nResidual-gap sweep: no interface, install path, provider environment, legacy vocabulary, held-out test tier, escalation compromise, or deferred blocking review finding remains undispositioned. Shell snippets remain only where provider configuration requires them and are validated test artifacts; application behavior remains Python-owned."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786445856730579001,"uuid":"df01b0f9-f768-4e65-95e8-f01c7603816a"}